1. Framework
A framework is a collection of pre-written code that helps developers build applications faster.

Examples:
- Spring Framework (Java)
- Django (Python)
- Angular (JavaScript)

2. Why Spring?
Before Spring, Java development relied on Servlets, JSP, and EJB, which required lots of configuration and were difficult to maintain.

3. Spring Framework
Spring is an open-source Java framework used to build web applications, REST APIs, enterprise applications, microservices, and cloud applications.

Key Features:
- Dependency Injection (DI)
- Inversion of Control (IoC)
- Spring MVC
- Spring Security
- Database Connectivity
- REST APIs
- Microservices

4. Dependency Injection (DI)
Instead of creating objects manually, Spring automatically provides required objects (dependencies).

Types of Dependency Injection
1. Constructor Injection

@Service
public class EmployeeService {
    private EmployeeRepository repository;
    @Autowired
    public EmployeeService(EmployeeRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}

Working :
Spring creates EmployeeRepository
          ↓
Spring calls constructor
          ↓
Passes EmployeeRepository object
          ↓
EmployeeService is ready

2. Setter Injection

@Service
public class EmployeeService {
    private EmployeeRepository repository;
    @Autowired
    public void setRepository(EmployeeRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }
}

Working :
Creates EmployeeService
        ↓
Calls setRepository()
        ↓
Injects EmployeeRepository

3. Field Injection

@Service
public class EmployeeService {
    @Autowired
    private EmployeeRepository repository;
}
--> Spring injects directly into the field.

5. Inversion of Control (IoC)
Spring controls object creation and management instead of the developer.

6. Spring Container
The core component responsible for:
- Creating objects
- Managing objects
- Injecting dependencies
- Destroying objects

7. Bean
Any object managed by the Spring Container is called a Bean.

8. Common Annotations
@Component - Create and manage this class as a Bean
@Service - Responsible for Calculations, Validations and Business rules
@Repository - Used for database operations
@Controller - Used in Spring MVC applications
@RestController - Returns JSON instead of HTML
@Autowired - Spring automatically injects the object
@Bean - Spring creates a Bean from this method
@Configuration - Used to create configuration classes. Think of it as Settings Class
@SpringBootApplication - It combines three annotations: @Configuration, @EnableAutoConfiguration and @ComponentScan
@ComponentScan - Tells Spring where to search for Beans like @Component, @Service, @Repository and @Controller
@RequestMapping - Maps URLs
@GetMapping - Used for GET requests
@PostMapping - Used to save data
@PutMapping - Used to update data
@DeleteMapping - Used to delete data
@PathVariable - Reads values from URL
@Entity - Used to create database tables
@OneToMany - One parent, many children
@ManyToOne - Many children belong to one parent

